# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-3.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: boethius-3.logic.r1 has nothing from it; boethius-3.logic.r1.2 has "good_ruler ∧ evil_exists → astonishing"; they differ when only "there exists a good ruler of the universe" and "evil is at all" are true, which the first allows; boethius-3.logic.r1.3 has "good_ruler ∧ ◇evil_exists ∧ evil_unpunished → astonishing"
boethius-3.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read boethius-3.logic.r1 and boethius-3.logic.r1.2 with no modality; boethius-3.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "must" does not say which it means
boethius-3.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps boethius-3.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "there exists a good ruler of the universe", "evil is at all" and "evil goes unpunished" are true and "this deservedly provokes astonishment" and "possibly, evil is at all" are false. Is S4, "Surely thou must see how deservedly this of itself provokes astonishment.", true then?
boethius-3.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: boethius-3.logic.r1 has "god_all"; boethius-3.logic.r1.2 and boethius-3.logic.r1.3 have "god_all" and "god_all ∧ wicked_flourish ∧ virtue_punished → beyond_wonder"; they differ when only "wickedness reigns and flourishes", "virtue lacks its reward, is trampled under the feet of the wicked, and suffers punishment in the place of crime" and "the rule is that of a God who knows all things and can do all things, but wills only the good" are true, which the first allows
boethius-3.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps boethius-3.logic.r1.2 and boethius-3.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "wickedness reigns and flourishes", "virtue lacks its reward, is trampled under the feet of the wicked, and suffers punishment in the place of crime" and "the rule is that of a God who knows all things and can do all things, but wills only the good" are true and "this cannot be sufficiently wondered at nor sufficiently lamented" is false. Is S6, "That this should happen under the rule of a God who knows all things and can do all things, but wills only the good, cannot be sufficiently wondered at nor sufficiently lamented.'", true then?
boethius-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: boethius-3.logic.r1 has "good_ruler ∧ evil_exists ∧ evil_unpunished → astonishing" and "god_all ∧ wicked_flourish ∧ virtue_punished → beyond_wonder"; boethius-3.logic.r1.2 and boethius-3.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "there exists a good ruler of the universe", "evil is at all", "evil goes unpunished", "this deservedly provokes astonishment", "wickedness reigns and flourishes", "virtue lacks its reward, is trampled under the feet of the wicked, and suffers punishment in the place of crime" and "the rule is that of a God who knows all things and can do all things, but wills only the good" are true, which the second allows
boethius-3.md:1: argue/negation: boethius-3.logic.r1 P3 (S6): the sentence says "nor", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "the rule is that of a God who knows all things and can do all things, but wills only the good"
